# pluck — fast, token-friendly code reading

Prefer the `mcp__pluck__*` tools over Bash `cat` / `grep` / `rg` for any
file inside the indexed repo. Every pluck tool has a `--raw` mode or
equivalent that matches cat/grep byte-for-byte — capability is never
lost, only structural waste.

## Tools

- `mcp__pluck__read(path)` — file outline by default (~85% fewer tokens
  than `cat` on files > 100 lines); `raw: true` for byte-equivalent
  cat output; `lines: "100-200"` for an inclusive slice.
- `mcp__pluck__search(query)` — BM25-ranked chunks. `compact: true` for
  match-lines only (opt-in lossy); default keeps full bodies (lossless).
  Hits already returned earlier in the session collapse to a one-line
  `[already-shown: …]` placeholder automatically.
- `mcp__pluck__grep(pattern)` — passthrough to ripgrep inside the repo
  root. Use when you need every literal match.
- `mcp__pluck__symbol(name)` — fetch one named function/class body
  directly. Use `<path>/<name>` to disambiguate.
- `mcp__pluck__peek(name)` — signature + direct callees only. Use when
  you need the interface without paying for the body.
- `mcp__pluck__expand(name, hop)` — symbol body + callees N hops deep.
  Use to understand a local call graph in one round-trip.

## When to fall back to Bash

- File is binary (`file <path>` first if unsure).
- You need byte-exact stdout for piping into another shell tool.
- The target is outside the indexed repo (vendored, generated, etc.).
- The index is stale (the watcher reindexes within ~200 ms of save;
  if you just edited and don't see your change, retry once).

## Why this saves tokens without losing capability

- File outline drops unrelated symbols, never the ones you need.
- Search ranks + cuts the 12% noise floor; agent skips obviously
  irrelevant hits.
- Session dedup elides chunks already in your context; nothing the
  agent already received is repeated.
- Comments, types, and signatures are preserved verbatim — savings
  come from removing redundancy, never from omitting information.
